Real code:

public class BankAccount {
    private int balance;

    public BankAccount(int balance) {
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public void withdraw(int amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        synchronized (this) {
            if (amount > balance) {
                return;
            }

            try {
                Thread.sleep(100);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                return;
            }

            balance = balance - amount;
        }
    }

    public int getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}

withdraw() is NOT marked synchronized - a `synchronized (this)` block is used inside it instead. What exactly does that block lock?,"The withdraw() method, so no thread anywhere can run it",The BankAccount object it was called on. Two threads working on two DIFFERENT accounts never wait for each other,The balance field only,"The whole BankAccount class, so all accounts are locked together",,2,`synchronized (this)` locks the current object. This is the same lock a `public synchronized void withdraw()` would have taken - the block form just lets you choose how much of the method it covers.,2

Real code:

public class BankAccount {
    private int balance;

    public BankAccount(int balance) {
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public void withdraw(int amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        synchronized (this) {
            if (amount > balance) {
                return;
            }

            try {
                Thread.sleep(100);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                return;
            }

            balance = balance - amount;
        }
    }

    public int getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}

The `amount <= 0` check is deliberately left OUTSIDE the block. Why was it written that way?,Because a check cannot be placed inside a synchronized block,"Because it only looks at the argument, not at any shared data. Nothing about it can go wrong between threads, so putting it inside would make threads wait for no reason",Because negative amounts must be rejected before the account is created,Because the lock cannot be taken until the amount is known to be valid,,2,"The lock only needs to cover shared, changing data. `amount` is a parameter - each thread has its own copy - so guarding it buys nothing and costs waiting time.",2

