public class BankAccount {
    private int balance;

    public BankAccount(int balance) {
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public void withdraw(int amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        synchronized (this) {
            if (amount > balance) {
                return;
            }

            try {
                Thread.sleep(100);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                return;
            }

            balance = balance - amount;
        }
    }

    public int getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}

The balance check and the subtraction are both inside the same block. Why must they be together rather than locking just the subtraction?,They do not have to be - locking the subtraction alone would be equally safe,"Because otherwise another thread can slip in between the check and the subtraction, so both threads pass a check against the old balance and both then deduct",Because the compiler requires the if and the assignment in the same block,Because sleep() can only be called inside a lock,,2,Check-then-act only works if the whole decision is one indivisible unit. This is the single most common way a lock gets added and still fails to fix anything.,2

