// a broken variant
public void withdraw(int amount) {
    if (amount <= 0) {
        return;
    }
    if (amount > balance) {       // check now OUTSIDE the lock
        return;
    }
    synchronized (this) {
        balance = balance - amount;
    }
}

One shared account holds 1000 and two threads each withdraw 700 using the broken variant. What can happen?,Nothing changes - the subtraction is still protected,"Both threads can pass the balance check while the balance is still 1000, then take turns subtracting, leaving -400. The lock protected the subtraction but not the decision to subtract",The second thread throws an exception when the balance would go negative,"The balance ends at 300, the same as the correct version",,2,Verified against a no-lock version of exactly this shape: 7 out of 8 runs ended at -400. Locking only the write is a very convincing-looking non-fix.,2

Real code:

public class BankAccount {
    private int balance;

    public BankAccount(int balance) {
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public void withdraw(int amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        synchronized (this) {
            if (amount > balance) {
                return;
            }

            try {
                Thread.sleep(100);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                return;
            }

            balance = balance - amount;
        }
    }

    public int getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}

Why is there a `Thread.sleep(100)` sitting inside the synchronized block? What is it actually for?,It gives the balance time to update before the subtraction,"It is there for the demo. It stretches out the time spent inside the critical section, so the race is easy to reproduce and another thread is visibly kept waiting - it is not part of the fix","It is required, because synchronized blocks must contain a blocking call",It prevents the two threads from starting at the same instant,,2,The sleep does not cause the bug and does not fix it. Real bugs like this hit inside a few nanoseconds and hide on a fast machine

Real code:

public class BankAccount {
    private int balance;

    public BankAccount(int balance) {
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public void withdraw(int amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        synchronized (this) {
            if (amount > balance) {
                return;
            }

            try {
                Thread.sleep(100);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                return;
            }

            balance = balance - amount;
        }
    }

    public int getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}

You delete the `Thread.sleep(100)` from the unsynchronized version of this code and the wrong balance stops appearing. What have you actually changed?,You have fixed the bug - no wrong balance means no problem,"Nothing about the bug. You have only made the dangerous window so small that you stop hitting it. The code is exactly as broken, it just now hides on this machine",You have made the code slower but safer,"You have removed the shared state, so no lock is needed",,2,This is why a threading bug cannot be dismissed because a few runs looked fine. Making a race harder to observe is the opposite of fixing it.,2

Real code:

// Main
BankAccount account1 = new BankAccount(1000);
BankAccount account2 = new BankAccount(1000);

Thread t1 = new Thread(new WithdrawTask(account1, 700), "T1");
Thread t2 = new Thread(new WithdrawTask(account2, 700), "T2");

t1.start();
t2.start();
t1.join();
t2.join();

System.out.println("Final balance: " + account1.getBalance());
System.out.println("Final balance: " + account2.getBalance());

Look carefully at what each thread is given. Does this demo actually exercise the `synchronized (this)` block?,Yes - two threads are running withdraw() at the same time,"No. T1 uses account1 and T2 uses account2, so they lock two different objects and never wait for each other. Each just withdraws 700 from its own 1000 and prints 300","Yes, because both threads use the same WithdrawTask class","No, because 700 is less than 1000",,2,"Verified output is 'Final balance: 300' twice, and it would be 300 twice even with no lock at all. To make the lock matter, BOTH threads have to be handed the SAME account.",2

