public class BankAccount {
    private int balance;

    public BankAccount(int balance) {
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public void withdraw(int amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        synchronized (this) {
            if (amount > balance) {
                return;
            }

            try {
                Thread.sleep(100);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                return;
            }

            balance = balance - amount;
        }
    }

    public int getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}

getBalance() has no lock at all. Why is that acceptable in this demo, and when would it stop being acceptable?,"It is always fine, because reading a value cannot break anything","It is fine only because Main calls it after join(), when no thread is still changing the balance. Called while the threads were running it could return a value from halfway through someone's withdrawal",It is fine because getBalance() returns a copy of the balance,It is never acceptable and the demo is simply wrong,,2,"The join() calls are doing the safety work, not the getter. A reader running alongside the writers needs the lock too.",2

Real code:

class Singleton {

    private static Singleton instance;

    private Singleton() {
    }

    public static Singleton getInstance() {

        synchronized (Singleton.class) {
            if (instance == null) {

                // Only to make the race condition easy to reproduce
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(100);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    e.printStackTrace();
                }

                instance = new Singleton();
            }

            return instance;
        }
    }
}

getInstance() locks on `Singleton.class` rather than on `this`. Why was it written that way?,Because locking the class is always faster than locking an object,"Because getInstance() is static, so there is no `this` to lock - and the data it protects is a static field shared by every caller, not something belonging to one object",Because Singleton has a private constructor,Because `this` would only lock the first instance created,,2,"Writing `synchronized (this)` inside a static method does not even compile. The lock has to match what it protects: static data needs a lock that is also shared program-wide, which is what the class object gives you.",2

