}

public class SingletonTask implements Runnable {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        Singleton instance = Singleton.getInstance();
        System.out.println(instance);
    }
}

Two threads call getInstance() at almost the same moment. With the lock in place, what does the program print?,"Two different Singleton identities, one per thread","The same Singleton identity twice - verified output was Singleton@72384b69 printed by both threads, meaning only one object was ever created","One line only, because the second thread is blocked forever",Nothing - the objects cannot be printed,,2,"Identical identities are the proof. If two objects had been created the two printed values would differ, which is exactly what happens without the lock.",2

Real code:

class Singleton {

    private static Singleton instance;

    private Singleton() {
    }

    public static Singleton getInstance() {

        synchronized (Singleton.class) {
            if (instance == null) {

                // Only to make the race condition easy to reproduce
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(100);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    e.printStackTrace();
                }

                instance = new Singleton();
            }

            return instance;
        }
    }
}

Suppose the `synchronized (Singleton.class)` line is removed. What can two threads produce, and why?,"The same object, because the null check still protects it","Two different Singleton objects. Both can find instance still null, both then create one, and each caller walks away with a different object - so a class that promises to have one instance now has two",A NullPointerException in the second thread,"One object, but the second thread gets null",,2,"Exactly the same check-then-act shape as the bank account, applied to deciding whether to create something. The sleep inside makes this easy to reproduce rather than a once-in-a-thousand-runs surprise.",2

Real code:

class Singleton {

    private static Singleton instance;

    private Singleton() {
    }

    public static Singleton getInstance() {

        synchronized (Singleton.class) {
            if (instance == null) {

                // Only to make the race condition easy to reproduce
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(100);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    e.printStackTrace();
                }

                instance = new Singleton();
            }

            return instance;
        }
    }
}

What is the comment 'Only to make the race condition easy to reproduce' telling you about the sleep?,That the sleep is needed for the singleton to work correctly,"That the sleep is demo scaffolding. It widens the gap between the null check and the assignment so two threads reliably land in it - real code would have no sleep and the race would just be rarer, not absent",That the sleep should be increased in production for safety,That the sleep replaces the need for a lock,,2,"Worth noting the author wrote that comment deliberately. Without it, someone reading the file later could easily assume the pause was doing something functional.",2

Real code:

class Singleton {

    private static Singleton instance;

    private Singleton() {
    }

    public static Singleton getInstance() {

        synchronized (Singleton.class) {
            if (instance == null) {

                // Only to make the race condition easy to reproduce
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(100);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    e.printStackTrace();
                }

                instance = new Singleton();
            }

            return instance;
        }
    }
}

Would `public static synchronized Singleton getInstance()` with no inner block behave the same as the current code?,No - a static synchronized method takes no lock at all,"Yes, essentially. A static synchronized method locks the class object, which is exactly what `synchronized (Singleton.class)` does here, and the block already wraps the whole method body",No - the method version would lock `this` instead,No - the method version would allow two instances to be created,,2,"Since the block covers the entire body, the two forms are equivalent. The block form is worth keeping when you later want to leave part of the method outside the lock, as the bank account does.",2

Real code:

class Singleton {

    private static Singleton instance;

    private Singleton() {
    }

    public static Singleton getInstance() {

        synchronized (Singleton.class) {
            if (instance == null) {

                // Only to make the race condition easy to reproduce
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(100);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    e.printStackTrace();
                }

                instance = new Singleton();
            }

            return instance;
        }
    }
}

`return instance;` sits INSIDE the synchronized block rather than after it. Does that matter?,Yes - returning from inside a lock leaves the lock permanently held,"No. The lock is released when the block is left, including by a return, so both placements are safe. Returning inside also guarantees you read the field while still holding the lock",Yes - the value returned would be null,"No, but only because the method is static",,2,"A lock is always released on the way out of the block, whether by falling off the end, returning, or an exception being thrown.",2

