public class ConfigLoader {

    private static Map<String, String> settings;

    public static Map<String, String> get() {
        synchronized (ConfigLoader.class) {
            if (settings == null) {
                settings = readFromDisk();      // slow, must happen only once
            }
            return settings;
        }
    }
}

ConfigLoader has the same shape as the Singleton but a different job. What is being protected, and is the class lock the right choice?,Nothing needs protecting - a Map is already thread-safe,"The static settings field, and yes. Two threads could both find it null and both read from disk - doing the slow work twice and each getting a different Map. Static data plus a static method means the class lock is the matching one","The disk file, and no - a file lock is needed instead","The return value, and no - the lock should be on the Map object",,2,"Same shape, different domain: lazy creation of shared data. Recognising the shape is what lets you reuse the fix without rediscovering it.",2

Real code:

public class IdGenerator {

    private static int lastId = 0;          // ONE counter for the whole program

    public int nextId() {
        synchronized (this) {               // locks this IdGenerator object
            lastId = lastId + 1;
            return lastId;
        }
    }
}

lastId is static - one counter for the whole program - but the lock is `synchronized (this)`. Two threads each hold their OWN IdGenerator and call nextId(). Is this safe?,Yes - the counter is protected by the synchronized block,"No. Each thread locks its own IdGenerator object, so neither ever waits for the other, and both can read and write the shared lastId at the same time - producing duplicate IDs","Yes, because static fields are automatically thread-safe","No, but only if there are more than two threads",,2,The most important mismatch to be able to spot: the lock is per-object while the data is per-class. A lock only helps if every thread touching the data takes the SAME lock.,2

Real code:

public class IdGenerator {

    private static int lastId = 0;          // ONE counter for the whole program

    public int nextId() {
        synchronized (this) {               // locks this IdGenerator object
            lastId = lastId + 1;
            return lastId;
        }
    }
}

public class IdGenerator {

    private static int lastId = 0;

    public int nextId() {
        synchronized (IdGenerator.class) {  // locks the class
            lastId = lastId + 1;
            return lastId;
        }
    }
}

The second version changes one thing: the lock is now on IdGenerator.class. Why does that fix it?,Because class locks are faster,"Because there is exactly one class object no matter how many IdGenerator instances exist, so every thread now competes for the same lock - which matches the fact that they all share the same static counter",Because it also makes lastId final,Because static methods can only use class locks,,2,"The rule that carries over: instance data goes with `this`, static data goes with `.class`. Match the lock to the scope of what it guards.",2

Real code:

public class BankAccount {
    private int balance;

    public BankAccount(int balance) {
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public void withdraw(int amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        synchronized (this) {
            if (amount > balance) {
                return;
            }

            try {
                Thread.sleep(100);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                return;
            }

            balance = balance - amount;
        }
    }

    public int getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}

// Main
BankAccount a = new BankAccount(1000);
BankAccount b = new BankAccount(1000);

Thread t1 = new Thread(new WithdrawTask(a, 700), "T1");
Thread t2 = new Thread(new WithdrawTask(b, 700), "T2");

T1 is inside its synchronized block on account a, holding the lock for 100 ms. Does T2 have to wait to enter its block on account b?,Yes - only one thread at a time may be inside a synchronized block,"No. The lock belongs to the object, and a and b are different objects, so the two threads proceed at the same time without ever meeting","Yes, because both are BankAccount objects",Only if a and b hold the same balance,,2,This is precisely why the class-42 demo shows no contention. It is also the answer to 'does adding synchronized slow everything down' - it only slows down threads competing for the SAME object.,2

Real code:

public class Meter {

    private static int totalReadings = 0;      // shared by every Meter
    private int myReadings = 0;                // this Meter only

    public void record() {
        synchronized (this) {
            myReadings = myReadings + 1;
        }
        synchronized (Meter.class) {
            totalReadings = totalReadings + 1;
        }
    }
}

record() takes two different locks in turn. Why does this class need both?,It does not - one of the two blocks is redundant,"Because it changes two different kinds of data: myReadings belongs to this one Meter so `this` is the right lock, while totalReadings is shared by every Meter so the class lock is the right one",Because taking two locks is always safer than one,Because static fields must be locked twice,,2,"A good illustration that the choice is not a style preference. Each piece of data has a scope, and the lock has to have the same scope.",2

Real code:

public class Meter {

    private static int totalReadings = 0;      // shared by every Meter
    private int myReadings = 0;                // this Meter only

    public void record() {
        synchronized (this) {
            myReadings = myReadings + 1;
        }
        synchronized (Meter.class) {
            totalReadings = totalReadings + 1;
        }
    }
}

Thread A is inside the `synchronized (this)` block of one Meter. Thread B tries to enter the `synchronized (Meter.class)` block. Does B wait?,"Yes - both blocks are in the same method, so they share a lock","No. An object lock and a class lock are two separate locks, so holding one does nothing to stop another thread taking the other","Yes, because Meter.class includes every Meter object",Only if A and B are working on the same Meter,,2,Worth being clear about: `this` and `.class` do not overlap at all. Guarding the same field with `this` in one method and `.class` in another gives you no protection whatsoever.,2

Real code:

public class Cart {

    private int itemCount = 0;
    private double total = 0.0;

    public void addItem(double price) {
        synchronized (this) {
            itemCount = itemCount + 1;
            total = total + price;
        }
    }

    public void clear() {
        synchronized (this) {
            itemCount = 0;
            total = 0.0;
        }
    }
}

Thread A is inside addItem() on a Cart. Thread B calls clear() on the SAME Cart. Does B wait?,"No - they are different methods, so they use different locks","Yes. Both blocks lock `this`, and it is the same object, so B waits until A leaves its block","No, because clear() only resets values","Yes, but only because both methods change two fields",,2,"There is one lock per object, not one per method. That is correct here - both methods change itemCount and total together, so they genuinely must not overlap.",2

Real code:

public class SeatBooking {

    private final boolean[] booked = new boolean[50];

    public boolean book(int seat, String customer) {
        synchronized (this) {
            if (booked[seat]) {
                return false;
            }
            booked[seat] = true;
            return true;
        }
    }
}

public class BookingTask implements Runnable {
    private final SeatBooking booking;
    private final String customer;

    public BookingTask(SeatBooking booking, String customer) {
        this.booking = booking;
        this.customer = customer;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        boolean got = booking.book(12, customer);
        System.out.println(customer + " got seat: " + got);
    }
}

Two threads try to book seat 12 at the same time on one SeatBooking object. With this lock in place, what gets printed?,"Both print got seat: true, because each ran book() successfully","One prints true and the other prints false. The check and the assignment are inside one lock, so the second thread only gets to look after the first has finished claiming the seat",Both print false,One prints true and the other throws an exception,,2,"Notice this is also better API design than the bank account's version: book() returns a boolean, so the caller can actually tell whether it worked instead of having to inspect state afterwards.",2

Real code:

public class Inventory {

    private int stock = 5;

    public boolean sell() {
        if (stock <= 0) {              // check is OUTSIDE the lock
            return false;
        }
        synchronized (this) {
            stock = stock - 1;         // only the change is locked
            return true;
        }
    }
}

There are 5 items in stock and 8 threads all call sell() at once. Look carefully at where the lock starts. What can go wrong?,"Nothing - the decrement is protected, so stock cannot go below zero","Stock can go negative. All 8 threads can pass the `stock <= 0` check while stock is still 5, and then each takes its turn decrementing - so more items are sold than exist",Some threads get an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException,"Exactly 5 succeed, because the lock counts the sales",,2,"The identical mistake as the broken bank variant, in a different domain. The lock has to start BEFORE the check that the change depends on.",2

Real code:

public class Account {

    private int balance;

    public Account(int balance) {
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public void transferTo(Account other, int amount) {
        synchronized (this) {
            if (amount > balance) {
                return;
            }
            balance = balance - amount;
            other.deposit(amount);      // touches the OTHER account
        }
    }

    public void deposit(int amount) {
        synchronized (this) {
            balance = balance + amount;
        }
    }
}

transferTo() locks `this` - the sending account - and then calls other.deposit(amount). Is the receiving account's balance protected while all this happens?,"Yes - the synchronized block covers everything inside it, including the deposit","Only during deposit() itself, which takes the receiver's own lock. The sender's lock says nothing about the receiver, so another thread can be changing the receiver's balance in between","Yes, because both accounts are Account objects and share a class lock","No, and the fix is to remove the lock from deposit()",,2,A lock only ever protects what other threads also lock. `synchronized (this)` in the sender covers the sender's balance

Real code:

public class TaxRates {

    private final Map<String, Double> rates = new HashMap<>();

    public TaxRates() {
        rates.put("food", 0.05);
        rates.put("luxury", 0.28);
    }

    // nothing ever calls put() after the constructor
    public double rateFor(String category) {
        return rates.get(category);
    }
}

Several threads call rateFor() at the same time, and nothing ever calls put() after the constructor. Does this need a synchronized block?,Yes - every shared object needs a lock,"No. The map never changes after it is built, and threads only reading unchanging data cannot interfere. A lock here would only add waiting for no benefit","Yes, because HashMap is not thread-safe",Only the constructor needs to be synchronized,,2,An important counter-example. Shared data is not automatically a critical section - the danger comes from shared data that CHANGES. Two ingredients are needed: sharing AND modification.,2

Real code:

public class BankAccount {
    private int balance;

    public BankAccount(int balance) {
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public void withdraw(int amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        synchronized (this) {
            if (amount > balance) {
                return;
            }

            try {
                Thread.sleep(100);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                return;
            }

            balance = balance - amount;
        }
    }

    public int getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}

Does adding this synchronized block make the program faster or slower?,"Faster, because threads stop interfering with each other",Slower when threads compete for the same object - they take turns instead of running together. You accept that because the alternative is a wrong balance,No difference either way,"Faster on several cores, slower on one",,2,"synchronized is a correctness tool with a cost, not an optimisation. That is why the cheap `amount <= 0` check was deliberately left outside it.",2

Real code:

public class BankAccount {
    private int balance;

    public BankAccount(int balance) {
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public void withdraw(int amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        synchronized (this) {
            if (amount > balance) {
                return;
            }

            try {
                Thread.sleep(100);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                return;
            }

            balance = balance - amount;
        }
    }

    public int getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}

The code sleeps for 100 ms while holding the lock. Why is that fine here but a bad habit in real code?,It is fine in both cases - locks are meant to be held during slow work,"Here it is deliberate, so the race and the waiting are visible. In real code you never do anything slow while holding a lock - every other thread is stuck waiting while nothing useful happens",It is wrong in both cases - the demo should not sleep at all,It is fine in real code as long as the pause is under a second,,2,"Say this out loud whenever the demo is shown, or the pattern gets copied into something that matters. Slow work - sleeping, network calls, disk reads - belongs outside the locked region.",2
